WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.068 size:88% position:44%
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-winning actor

00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:03.670 size:81% position:41%
you know from movies
such as "The Birdcage"

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:05.171 align:start size:94% position:6%
and shows like "Ray Donovan,
"Brockmire,"

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:08.641 align:start size:94% position:6%
and his three decades-long run
on "The Simpsons."

00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:12.379 align:start size:94% position:6%
He is the host of
"The Jim Brockmire Podcast,"

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:13.179 line:92%
with episodes available now.

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:15.715 align:start size:94% position:6%
Please welcome back to the sho
Hank Azaria.

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:17.283 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
How are you, Hank?

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:19.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Hello, Seth.
Nice to see you.

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:21.721 align:start size:94% position:6%
We're both in our casual
button-down shirts

00:00:21.721 --> 00:00:22.722 size:84% position:42% line:92%
with sleeves rolled up.

00:00:22.722 --> 00:00:24.391 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is a dream come true
really.

00:00:24.391 --> 00:00:26.226 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's not bad. I --
-No.

00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:27.660 line:92%
-I want to talk to you about

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.762 align:start size:94% position:6%
how you figured out
working from home,

00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:32.699
but I also enjoy talking to yo
about baseball and the Mets.

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:35.802 size:88% position:44%
We're talking right now,
the Mets are above .500.

00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:38.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
I know it's early,
but how are you feeling?

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:42.308 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, well, cautious.
I'm feeling cautious, Seth

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:43.743 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm very used to getting
my heart broken.

00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:46.913 align:start size:94% position:6%
Something usually goes
terribly wrong, as we know

00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:48.114 line:92%
As Jim Brockmire likes to say,

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:51.818
"the Mets are the Yankees
of not being the Yankees."

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:54.521 align:start size:94% position:6%
But right now --
Well, the Yankees are so bad

00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:56.222 align:start size:94% position:6%
that that's actually working
in the Mets' favor.

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:58.691 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I -- You know,
you have a nagging feeling

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:00.460 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the universe
will right itself

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:01.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the Yankees
will start winning

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:06.900
and the Mets will fall apart
in some new and amazing way.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.135 align:start size:94% position:6%
My money is on
Bernie Madoff's ghost

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:11.838
doing a reverse, like, "Angels
in the Outfield" kind of thing

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:14.340
-It'd be nice to get Berni
back involved with the team.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.876 size:84% position:42%
I know he had a special
connection with them

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:17.710 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
over the years.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.479 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, he's been great
to all Mets fans.

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:23.450
Just like his -- It was like his
specter will shove Jacob deGro

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:24.484 line:92%
and, like, pick up an outfielder

00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:28.154 align:start size:94% position:6%
and replace him with Tim Tebow
or something.

00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:28.855 size:81% position:41% line:92%
-You -- I -- You know,

00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:32.392
this is such a nice outcom
for your character Jim Brockmire

00:01:32.392 --> 00:01:37.163
because, as we've talked before,
he sort of started as a voic

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:40.433 align:start size:94% position:6%
before he became anything
that we could see.

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:43.303
And, so, now he is back to
being a voice on a podcast

00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:48.708 align:start size:94% position:6%
You must enjoy the real estate
that a podcast allows you

00:01:48.708 --> 00:01:52.512 align:start size:94% position:6%
to have with this incredible
character you created.

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:52.946 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-I do.

00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:55.748 align:start size:94% position:6%
[As Jim] Any excuse to continu
to talk this way, Seth,

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:57.183 size:75% position:38% line:92%
is good news for me.

00:01:57.183 --> 00:02:00.520
This is the generic baseball
announcer voice of the 1970s

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:03.189 align:start size:94% position:6%
that is also
Jim Brockmire's voice.

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:04.657 align:start size:94% position:6%
[Normal voice]
You know, it really --

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:07.160
This podcast grew out of the way
we promoted the show

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.429 align:start size:94% position:6%
over the years
even before it was a series.

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:12.799 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'd go on "The Rich Eisen Show
as Brockmire

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:15.969
and all these ESPN shows,
and they're fun to go on

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:18.304
because you get a very
knowledgeable sports commentator

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:21.307 align:start size:94% position:6%
giving an informed take
on whatever story of the day

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:24.544
and then Brockmire would come in
and give his take.

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:27.947 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was like putting
a drunk conspiracy theoris

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:29.415 align:start size:94% position:6%
on "Meet the Press"
or something.

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:32.485
And he has real sports opinions,
but they're insane,

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:36.055
and giving him a platform
is fairly irresponsible.

00:02:36.055 --> 00:02:37.857 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, you know,
it worked as promotion,

00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:40.226 align:start size:94% position:6%
so we figured
why not reverse it?

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:42.862 size:81% position:41%
-You -- You have a lot
of wonderful guests.

00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:47.667
Your first guest was someone
who is maybe as outlandish

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:50.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
as the fictional character
of Jim Brockmire,

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:52.205 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that person
was Charles Barkley.

00:02:52.205 --> 00:02:56.209 align:start size:94% position:6%
Was that as enjoyable to d
as it was to listen to?

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:57.277 line:92%
-It was completely surreal

00:02:57.277 --> 00:02:59.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
As you say,
Brockmire is outrageous,

00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:02.015 align:start size:94% position:6%
but he always tells the truth,
Brockmire.

00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:04.417 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's usually wasted
out of his mind

00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:06.286 align:start size:94% position:6%
or talking about
how he was wasted.

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:08.755 size:75% position:38%
And he says anything
on or off the air.

00:03:08.755 --> 00:03:11.858 align:start size:94% position:6%
So most guests are a littl
kind of shocked or taken aback

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:15.195 size:81% position:41%
by that, but Barkley
was completely immune.

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:17.397 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I realized
like 15 minutes in

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:21.034 align:start size:94% position:6%
that Barkley's more Brockmir
than Brockmire.

00:03:21.034 --> 00:03:22.402 line:92%
You know? Couldn't shock him

00:03:22.402 --> 00:03:25.572 size:78% position:39%
Whatever insane story
I told as Brockmire,

00:03:25.572 --> 00:03:28.308 align:start size:94% position:6%
Charles had one
like twice as crazy.

00:03:28.308 --> 00:03:31.077 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, yeah,
the interview went well.

00:03:31.077 --> 00:03:34.814 align:start size:94% position:6%
Charles actually
genuinely opened up.

00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:36.683 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't think he realized
that Brockmire

00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:37.717 line:92%
was a fictional character.

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:40.587 size:88% position:44%
And he's like, "Finally,
someone who understands

00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:42.422 align:start size:94% position:6%
where I'm coming from
when I talk about

00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:46.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
getting drunk and naked
and pretending to know karate,

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:47.694 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is a story
he actually told.

00:03:47.694 --> 00:03:53.166
-My -- I've been lucky enoug
to spend some time with Charles.

00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:56.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the best thing
about his stories,

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:58.271 size:75% position:38%
which are all better
than my best story,

00:03:58.271 --> 00:04:04.344 align:start size:94% position:6%
is he never tells them
like he's shocked it happened.

00:04:04.344 --> 00:04:05.878 align:start size:94% position:6%
-No. Not at all.
-Yeah.

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:07.547 align:start size:94% position:6%
-A couple of the stories
he told, Seth,

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:10.850 align:start size:94% position:6%
if we put those out,
he'd be canceled,

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:11.918 line:92%
I'd be canceled for listening.

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:13.920
You'd be canceled 'cause
I just told you about it.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.054 align:start size:94% position:6%
It'd be like that movie
"The Ring."

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:19.626 align:start size:94% position:6%
Anybody who heard it would
get canceled within seven days

00:04:19.626 --> 00:04:20.693 align:start size:94% position:6%
-He's a wonderful guy.
It was great

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:22.862 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you had him on.
You have a lot of great guests

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:24.497 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I also want
to congratulate you.

00:04:24.497 --> 00:04:27.900 line:92%
34 seasons of "The Simpsons.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.569 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-You know, people say

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:31.871
at that point
you must be able to phone it in.

00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:36.509
I know you don't do that insofar
as effort, but you do literall

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:39.646 size:81% position:41%
phone it in right now
due to pandemic rules.

00:04:39.646 --> 00:04:40.880 line:92%
How has that been? There -

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.782 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you just do it on the phone
-No.

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:45.084 align:start size:94% position:6%
They sent a lot of
professional equipment.

00:04:45.084 --> 00:04:47.420 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so we do it from home now.
It's gotten easier.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.857
But there have been times
when not only on "The Simpsons,"

00:04:50.857 --> 00:04:55.028
but, you know, you just throw it
on the voice memo for movies

00:04:55.028 --> 00:04:55.995 size:88% position:44% line:92%
or TV shows or whatever.

00:04:55.995 --> 00:04:58.631 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they'll forget
that it was just a voice memo,

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:02.435 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it will actually end u
in the show.

00:05:02.435 --> 00:05:03.102 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
And, you know...

00:05:03.102 --> 00:05:05.638
[As Chief Wiggum] "If you listen
closely to Chief Wiggum,

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:08.241 align:start size:94% position:6%
like, at timecode, like,
1 minute, 42 seconds,

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:10.943 size:78% position:39%
you can actually hear
the very faint sound

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:13.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
of my cat vomiting
in the distance.

00:05:13.212 --> 00:05:15.415 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's beautiful."
-Obviously, you've created

00:05:15.415 --> 00:05:17.784 size:81% position:41%
all these characters.
How many of the voices

00:05:17.784 --> 00:05:20.853 align:start size:94% position:6%
were based on
either celebrity impressions

00:05:20.853 --> 00:05:25.892 align:start size:94% position:6%
or people you had met
in your life up to that point?

00:05:25.892 --> 00:05:26.592 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-All of them.

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:29.462 align:start size:94% position:6%
All the voices are some form
of mimicry or another,

00:05:29.462 --> 00:05:33.433 align:start size:94% position:6%
either celebrities or people
I went to college with

00:05:33.433 --> 00:05:34.967 line:92%
or grew up with or whatever.

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:36.969 align:start size:94% position:6%
And some are, like,
good impressions,

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:38.471 align:start size:94% position:6%
and some are
horrible impressions,

00:05:38.471 --> 00:05:42.642 align:start size:94% position:6%
but either way, they make
sort of a good character voice

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:47.947
Sometimes I develop impression
out of, like, emotional need

00:05:47.947 --> 00:05:49.349 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Like, um...

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:51.884
Like, I gained a bunch of weight
about 20 years ago.

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:55.388
I gained like 35 pounds.
And I felt -- I felt fat.

00:05:55.388 --> 00:05:59.158 align:start size:94% position:6%
I felt heavy
'cause I kind of was, and...

00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:00.793 align:start size:94% position:6%
[As Marlon Brando]
"So I kind of --

00:06:00.793 --> 00:06:02.395 line:92%
I developed a Brando impressio

00:06:02.395 --> 00:06:06.666 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I just felt
like Marlon -- Marlon Brando

00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:09.135 line:92%
And I would call, and I'm --

00:06:09.135 --> 00:06:10.536 line:92%
'Can I get an extra large

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:12.438 align:start size:94% position:6%
stuffed-crust
meat lover's pizza?

00:06:12.438 --> 00:06:15.041 align:start size:94% position:6%
And someday --
and that day may never come --

00:06:15.041 --> 00:06:18.111 align:start size:94% position:6%
I may call upon you
for a second pizza.'"

00:06:18.111 --> 00:06:19.612 line:92%
You know, stuff like that.

00:06:19.612 --> 00:06:21.681 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You -- I always
am fascinated by the thing

00:06:21.681 --> 00:06:25.084 align:start size:94% position:6%
that happens to people who are
very good at impressions,

00:06:25.084 --> 00:06:26.886 line:92%
is sometimes they cross path

00:06:26.886 --> 00:06:30.123 align:start size:94% position:6%
with someone
that they've impersonated.

00:06:30.123 --> 00:06:30.690 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:34.761
-You -- You actually got t
sit down with Jack Nicholson

00:06:34.761 --> 00:06:38.331
who is someone that you were
known to have an impression of

00:06:38.331 --> 00:06:42.735 align:start size:94% position:6%
Did he want --
Did he know you did him?

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:43.336 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-No, he did not.

00:06:43.336 --> 00:06:47.774 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I had tremendous, like
social anxiety that he would -

00:06:47.774 --> 00:06:48.975 line:92%
that somebody would say it

00:06:48.975 --> 00:06:51.778 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he would ask me
for the impression.

00:06:51.778 --> 00:06:53.713 align:start size:94% position:6%
But the thing --
I actually had to sit

00:06:53.713 --> 00:06:56.682 size:81% position:41%
next to him on a plane
for a bunch of hours.

00:06:56.682 --> 00:06:58.951 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I was really --
I don't know --

00:06:58.951 --> 00:07:01.487 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't know if this ever --
I mean, your job

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:03.689 align:start size:94% position:6%
is you talk to --
and you have for many years --

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:05.024 align:start size:94% position:6%
talk to every manner
of celebrity.

00:07:05.024 --> 00:07:07.860 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't know if you get nervou
doing it ever still.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:11.297 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think I would get nervous
about a full flight.

00:07:11.297 --> 00:07:12.432 size:78% position:39% line:92%
You know what I mean?

00:07:12.432 --> 00:07:13.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's a whole different
set of stakes.

00:07:13.866 --> 00:07:16.002 size:78% position:39%
-Yeah, you're used to
15-minute increments.

00:07:16.002 --> 00:07:18.271 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Right.
-Yeah, so I'm really nervous

00:07:18.271 --> 00:07:21.073 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've got to make small tal
with Jack Nicholson.

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:23.276 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I had no reason
to be nervous 'cause...

00:07:23.276 --> 00:07:25.445 align:start size:94% position:6%
[As Nicholson] "Jack, I don'
know if you talked to him

00:07:25.445 --> 00:07:28.815
or met him --
By the way, this is lately Jack.

00:07:28.815 --> 00:07:32.084
[Softly] There's young Jac
that's kind of like this.

00:07:32.084 --> 00:07:34.787 align:start size:94% position:6%
[Loudly]
But this is more recent Jack

00:07:34.787 --> 00:07:38.024 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he's just happy
to talk about whatever,

00:07:38.024 --> 00:07:39.792 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he keeps it going
and just --

00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:42.895 align:start size:94% position:6%
You don't have to worry
about awkward pauses, Seth

00:07:42.895 --> 00:07:47.333
'cause he'll just fill it
the hell in, God damn it."

00:07:47.333 --> 00:07:51.003 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he has --
he has this crazy thing.

00:07:51.003 --> 00:07:54.707 align:start size:94% position:6%
He'll reference people,
friends of his or something,

00:07:54.707 --> 00:07:56.108 line:92%
that he assumes you know,

00:07:56.108 --> 00:08:00.379
and then he'll throw in, like,
little sayings that I can't --

00:08:00.379 --> 00:08:04.984
couldn't tell whether they wer
literal or metaphorical like..

00:08:04.984 --> 00:08:07.053 align:start size:94% position:6%
[As Nicholson]
"Well, you know, Seth,

00:08:07.053 --> 00:08:08.821 line:92%
it's just like Dicky D says.

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:11.090 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, if it ain't
pancakes in the mornin',

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:14.293 align:start size:94% position:6%
nobody's pouring syrup
in the afternoon."

00:08:14.293 --> 00:08:17.230 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you're like, "I'm sorry.
Who's Dicky D?"

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:19.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
[As Nicholson]
"Oh, Dicky D's my accountant

00:08:19.866 --> 00:08:21.234 size:78% position:39% line:92%
Known him for years."

00:08:21.234 --> 00:08:23.002 line:92%
"You mean literally pancakes?"

00:08:23.002 --> 00:08:25.671 align:start size:94% position:6%
[As Nicholson] "No, yeah.
I'm saying we had breakfast.

00:08:25.671 --> 00:08:27.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we wanted to have
some panc--"

00:08:27.039 --> 00:08:30.877 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm like, "Oh, okay."
But, you know, he doesn't --

00:08:30.877 --> 00:08:34.947
Whether it's literal or no
or whether you ask or not,

00:08:34.947 --> 00:08:36.148 line:92%
he's happy just to keep going.

00:08:36.148 --> 00:08:39.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
He'll explain it
or he'll just plow through

00:08:39.085 --> 00:08:40.553 line:92%
It was a delight actually.

00:08:40.553 --> 00:08:42.054 line:92%
-That is -- That is fantastic.

00:08:42.054 --> 00:08:43.789 line:92%
And I think that's a whole

00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:45.191 align:start size:94% position:6%
different class
of impressionist

00:08:45.191 --> 00:08:47.627 align:start size:94% position:6%
that can do the same perso
